\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Recovery of clean-code performance under obfuscation.} Every cell is \emph  {output prediction}\,/\,\emph  {input prediction}; backward is graded by execution. \textbf  {The reference is the shaded column}: the untuned model's accuracy on \texttt  {L0}, the unobfuscated program. For each arm the first column is its raw accuracy on the condition group and the second is that accuracy \textbf  {as a percentage of the shaded reference} --- \textbf  {100 means the arm recovers all of the untuned model's clean-code accuracy}, and below 100 means some of it is still lost to the obfuscation. The denominator is the same for every row of a model, so the \texttt  {base} column shows how far each condition group degrades the untuned model, and the remaining columns show how much of that each adaptation buys back. \textcolor {green!55!black}{Above} and \textcolor {red!70!black}{below} 100 are coloured; \textbf  {bold} marks the best arm in that row and direction. Rows are the condition groups of Appendix\nonbreakingspace \ref {app:absmaster}: \emph  {single} the seen transforms alone, \emph  {held-out} the unseen family, \emph  {d2--d4} stacks of that depth built only from seen transforms, \emph  {d2u}/\emph  {d3u} stacks containing the unseen family. The reference is \emph  {not} subject to the 0.25 format gate that excludes cells from means --- that gate keeps unreliable measurements out of an average, whereas a denominator is a different object, and untuned format failure reflects adherence to the prompt contract rather than capability (Section\nonbreakingspace \ref {sec:setup}) --- but a reference whose own rate exceeds the gate carries $\dagger  $, so a soft denominator is visible. StarCoder2-15B's untuned forward reading is not merely gated but zero, so it has no forward reference at all. \emph  {breadth} and \emph  {merge} are exactly data-matched; \emph  {router} carries two further experts.}}{21}{table.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{4}{21}{\textbf {Recovery of clean-code performance under obfuscation.} Every cell is \emph {output prediction}\,/\,\emph {input prediction}; backward is graded by execution. \textbf {The reference is the shaded column}: the untuned model's accuracy on \texttt {L0}, the unobfuscated program. For each arm the first column is its raw accuracy on the condition group and the second is that accuracy \textbf {as a percentage of the shaded reference} --- \textbf {100 means the arm recovers all of the untuned model's clean-code accuracy}, and below 100 means some of it is still lost to the obfuscation. The denominator is the same for every row of a model, so the \texttt {base} column shows how far each condition group degrades the untuned model, and the remaining columns show how much of that each adaptation buys back. \textcolor {green!55!black}{Above} and \textcolor {red!70!black}{below} 100 are coloured; \textbf {bold} marks the best arm in that row and direction. Rows are the condition groups of Appendix~\ref {app:absmaster}: \emph {single} the seen transforms alone, \emph {held-out} the unseen family, \emph {d2--d4} stacks of that depth built only from seen transforms, \emph {d2u}/\emph {d3u} stacks containing the unseen family. The reference is \emph {not} subject to the 0.25 format gate that excludes cells from means --- that gate keeps unreliable measurements out of an average, whereas a denominator is a different object, and untuned format failure reflects adherence to the prompt contract rather than capability (Section~\ref {sec:setup}) --- but a reference whose own rate exceeds the gate carries $\dagger $, so a soft denominator is visible. StarCoder2-15B's untuned forward reading is not merely gated but zero, so it has no forward reference at all. \emph {breadth} and \emph {merge} are exactly data-matched; \emph {router} carries two further experts}{table.caption.13}{}}
