\newlabel{tab:merge_variants_granite318b}{{21}{40}{\textbf {Merge variants against the untuned model, Granite-3.1-8B.} Each cell is \emph {output prediction}\,/\,\emph {input prediction}, raw exact-match accuracy; backward is graded by execution. The three merges combine the same six specialists with the same uniform weights, density $0.5$ and sign rule, and differ only in the sparsification step: \emph {TIES} keeps the largest entries by magnitude (Eq.~\ref {eq:trim}), \emph {DARE-TIES} drops entries at random and rescales (Eq.~\ref {eq:dare}), and \emph {DARE-linear} applies that drop without the sign election of Eq.~\ref {eq:elect}. \textbf {Colour}: \textcolor {green!55!black}{above} or \textcolor {red!70!black}{below} \texttt {base} in the same direction; a red number carrying a dagger is gated instead (format-failure rate above 0.25). \textbf {Bold rows} are group means. \emph {DARE-linear} was run forward only, so its backward half is absent by construction rather than by omission}{table.caption.32}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Master grid, CodeLlama-7B.} Every condition and every stack, forward (output prediction) and backward (input prediction). Per system: \emph  {acc} raw accuracy; $\Delta $ points against the untuned model on the same condition; \% accuracy as a percentage of the untuned model's clean-code accuracy in the same direction, i.e.\ how much of the original accuracy the system brings back (100 = the untuned model on clean code). \emph  {fmt}: format-failure rate above 0.25, the cell measures the prompt contract rather than the task and enters no mean. Pooled rows average the stacks above them. `--': cell not run.}}{41}{table.caption.33}\protected@file@percent }
